Clare says:
“I believe in strong communities, reliable public transport and well-funded local services.”
In Denby Dale  this means:
 • ensuring that housing developments reflect the preferences of local people, using brownfield sites and eyesores first; protecting our local amenities and green spaces and not over-stretching local services;
• pushing for enough energy efficient starter and retirement homes so local residents can access affordable housing suitable for their needs without moving out of the village;
• increasing the frequency of buses to town so we needn’t rely on cars to get to work, school and larger shops;
• doing everything possible to keep local amenities  open in the villages.

Clare Walters
has lived in Kirklees for nearly 30 years and her 4 children went to Shelley pyramid schools. She worked in nature conservation for 15 years and now works from home, training practitioners in health and wellbeing. She has a strong interest in biodynamic agriculture. 
Contacts: 07984 115927    clare.walters@absolute-specialists.co.uk

As a retired Police Detective, businessman and former council worker I’m committed to:
•	Making our city safer;
•	Providing housing for Milton Keynes citizens and our Military Veterans first;
•	Addressing antisocial behaviour;
•	Helping small businesses; and
•	Improving our current infrastructure before allowing anymore expansion. 
I will listen to residents and work hard for our community because your voice matters and deserves to be heard.

Only a vote for Reform UK will bring real change to our unique city.

If elected onto Suffolk County Council I promise to:
1. Hold Suffolk Highways to account to prioritise addressing the terrible state of our roads, sort out potholes, improve & maintain better the road surface, review and improve road markings, and clean road signs;
2. Fight for increased and improved local SEND education provision especially at primary school age, as there is too limited number of places, leaving children barred from education. By improving this local provision this will save taxpayers's money by reducing the transport bill;
3. Improve the provision and support available from both adult & children social services; and
4. Improve local bus services in/around the town, to encourage use of local businesses and also reduce the number of cars in town, which may help with the ongoing parking issues.
